---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProductBar from '../components/khensu/ProductBar.astro';
import { PRODUCTS } from '../data/hosts';

const ESSAY = '/mirrora/mirrora.pdf';
const DROPBOX =
  'https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/fzm73lnqjdw5hii32pc4o/M-R-R-mirrora.pdf?rlkey=073sge5x7lhbnfbjf0j6x18p9&st=10c5mpjw&dl=0';
---

<Base
  title="mîřrørå"
  description="mîřrørå — Elliott’s Mirror, quantum mirror theory. Many dimensions, one instance: a generic object with many pointers. Each pointer is an atman, and they are the same atman."
>
  <div class="khensu mr-page" id="mr-root">
    <ProductBar name="mîřrørå" action="The essay" href="#essay-section" explore="#shapes-section" />

    <section class="mr-hero" data-hero id="mirror-section">
      <div class="wrap mr-hero-grid">
        <div class="mr-hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Elliott’s Mirror · quantum mirror theory</p>
          <h1>
            mîřrørå<br />
            <em>One instance.</em>
          </h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Many dimensions are one object. The quon is that object. Every atman is a pointer to it, and every atman is
            the same atman. You are a deep link to the same app.
          </p>
          <div class="btn-row">
            <a class="btn primary" href="#essay-section">The essay</a>
            <a class="btn" href="#shapes-section">3-sphere · tesseract</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="mr-stage mr-hero-stage">
          <canvas id="mr-pointers" aria-label="One glowing quon in the centre, with named pointers around it."></canvas>
          <figcaption>One allocation. Many names. Change the object and every pointer sees it, because there is only one object.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="quon-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">the quon</p>
        <h2>
          Many names.<br />
          <em>One memory.</em>
        </h2>
        <p>
          mirrora is a quantum mirror theory. Worlds that look separate are one generic object with many pointers. Each
          pointer is an atman. Each atman is the same atman.
        </p>
        <p>
          The one object is the <strong>quon</strong> — the singularity. The PDF also writes QUOS, said “qu-ose”, like
          cosmos. Zero, here, is a flipped-off mirror attribute on a given axis. The maths of 0 and 1 stay. The claim is
          about identity.
        </p>
        <pre class="mr-code" tabindex="0"><code>type Quon = {'{'} … {'}'}          // the one instance
const you: Atman = quon
const me: Atman = quon
// same memory. different names.</code></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="indra-section">
      <div class="wrap mr-split">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">not îňðřæ.ōġ</p>
          <h2>
            Jewels reflect.<br />
            <em>Pointers alias.</em>
          </h2>
          <p>
            <a href={PRODUCTS.indra}>îňðřæ.ōġ</a> is Indra’s net: a jewel at every knot, each one holding a reflection of
            every other. That is interdependence by copy. mirrora is aliasing. One memory, many names. If Indra’s net is
            the network card, mirrora is the type system under it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="mr-cards two">
          <article>
            <h3>Indra’s net</h3>
            <p>Every jewel contains every other jewel. Reflections of reflections.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>mirrora</h3>
            <p>Every atman is a pointer to the same quon. No second object to reflect.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section band" id="shapes-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">S³ and the tesseract</p>
        <h2>
          Four numbers that sum to one.<br />
          <em>A cube of cubes.</em>
        </h2>
        <p>
          A 3-sphere is the surface of a 4-ball: <code>x² + y² + z² + w² = 1</code>. What you see is a stereographic
          projection — a globe flattened to a map, one dimension up —           while it turns through the hidden <code>w</code> axis. Every circle is a Hopf fibre: a perfect circle in 4D that never touches another, yet every pair is linked
          once.
        </p>
        <p>
          A tesseract is the same move for a cube. Sixteen corners, thirty-two edges. Rotate it through <code>w</code> and a cube turns inside-out without tearing. Same object. Another pointer.
        </p>
        <div class="mr-pair">
          <figure class="mr-stage">
            <canvas id="mr-sphere" aria-label="Animated 3-sphere. Hopf fibres, stereographically projected. Drag to turn."></canvas>
            <figcaption>3-sphere · Hopf fibres. Drag to turn.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="mr-stage">
            <canvas id="mr-tesseract" aria-label="Animated tesseract, a 4D hypercube projected to the screen. Drag to turn."></canvas>
            <figcaption>Tesseract · hypercube. Drag to turn.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="time-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">time, like space</p>
        <h2>
          Three time axes.<br />
          <em>One now.</em>
        </h2>
        <p>
          Space already duplicates one line three times: <code>x</code>, <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> are the same
          type. Time can do the same. <code>t</code>, <code>τ</code>, <code>σ</code> are three time axes of one time
          type. The cubes on those axes are not copies of a moment. They are pointers to the same now.
        </p>
        <div class="mr-stage tall">
          <canvas id="mr-time" aria-label="Three time axes meeting at one now."></canvas>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="essay-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">the essay</p>
        <h2>
          Written in one night.<br />
          <em>18–19 October 2026.</em>
        </h2>
        <p>
          Elliott wrote mîřrørå in Venice Beach, mid-Matrix, between meditation and physics. The essay is here as a PDF,
          and on Dropbox. It does not ask you to throw out the maths. It asks you to see many worlds as one object with
          many names — and then to make this dimension kinder, with <a href={PRODUCTS.bi}>nu.bi</a> as the safety net.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <a class="btn primary" href={ESSAY}>Read the essay</a>
          <a class="btn" href={DROPBOX}>Dropbox original</a>
          <a class="btn" href={PRODUCTS.indra}>îňðřæ.ōġ</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section band" id="life-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">related</p>
        <h2>
          Other creatures.<br />
          <em>The same pointer.</em>
        </h2>
        <p>
          Water bears, sulfur kitchens, jelly boxes and other chemistries are related pictures of Elliott’s Mirror.
          A paused body, a vent city and a cube of seawater are each one instance in another state.
        </p>

        <article class="mr-life">
          <h3>Water bears</h3>
          <p>
            Tardigrades enter cryptobiosis — the tun — and metabolism falls so far the clock itself pauses. Rehydrate
            them and the same animal walks. Recorded extremes include about −272.8 °C, above 100 °C, vacuum, organic
            solvents, 7.5 GPa, and gamma doses on the order of a thousand times a human lethal dose. In 2007, on
            FOTON-M3, they were the first animals to survive space vacuum, UV and cosmic radiation together, and then wake.
          </p>
          <div class="mr-cards">
            <article>
              <h3>The tun</h3>
              <p>Anhydrobiosis. Almost no water. A third state between life and death, reversible.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Space</h3>
              <p>FOTON-M3, 2007. Vacuum, UV, cosmic rays. Same water bear, later.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Pressure</h3>
              <p>7.5 GPa — deeper than the bottom of any ocean, and they still come back.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>The pointer</h3>
              <p>A paused body is the same atman, flipped to standby.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
          <figure class="mr-vid">
            <video src="/mirrora/tardigrade.mp4" poster="/mirrora/tardigrade.jpg" controls playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
            <figcaption>
              A tardigrade at about 100×. Dmitry Brant, <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tardigrade_in_real_time.ogv">Wikimedia Commons</a>, CC BY-SA 4.0.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <article class="mr-life">
          <h3>Sulfur and heat</h3>
          <p>
            At black smokers, chemolithoautotrophs oxidise hydrogen sulfide and feed whole cities of tube worms. The vent
            is the kitchen. <i>Geogemma barossii</i> (strain 121) grows at 121 °C. Antarctic brine keeps membranes fluid
            below 0 °C. The tun of a tardigrade has been taken near −273 °C.
          </p>
          <div class="mr-cards">
            <article>
              <h3>Sulfur</h3>
              <p>Hydrogen sulfide in, organic carbon out. The vent is a factory.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>strain 121</h3>
              <p>Strain 121 grows where we sterilise. Heat is a habitat.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Below zero</h3>
              <p>Psychrophiles in brine. The clock runs slow. It still runs.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
          <figure class="mr-vid">
            <video src="/mirrora/vent.mp4" poster="/mirrora/vent.jpg" controls playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
            <figcaption>
              An active hydrothermal vent, first imaged on the 2016 Deepwater Exploration of the Marianas.
              NOAA Ocean Exploration, public domain.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <article class="mr-life">
          <h3>Jelly boxes</h3>
          <p>
            Box jellyfish are cubes of jelly with twenty-four eyes. Deep-sea medusae, comb jellies, salps and pyrosomes
            are transparent boxes of seawater that swim. A cell is already a jelly box: a lipid bag around a reaction.
            Another chemistry still needs a compartment, a box that keeps the inside from becoming the ocean.
          </p>
          <figure class="mr-vid">
            <video src="/mirrora/jelly.mp4" poster="/mirrora/jelly.jpg" controls playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
            <figcaption>
              <i>Crossota millsae</i>, a deep-sea jelly at 1,015 m off Puerto Rico, tentacles out in every
              direction. NOAA Okeanos Explorer, public domain.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <article class="mr-life">
          <h3>Other chemistries</h3>
          <p>
            Silicon chains, ammonia as the solvent on Titan, sulfur lakes, fluorine or boron on paper — these are
            hypotheses. Earth’s extremes show how far carbon already goes: sulfur kitchens, ice brines, dry tuns, vacuum.
            Another chemistry, if it wakes, is another pointer to the same quon.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="sources-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ul class="mr-src">
          <li>Tardigrade film: Dmitry Brant, <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tardigrade_in_real_time.ogv">Tardigrade in real time</a>, CC BY-SA 4.0.</li>
          <li>Space survival: Jönsson, Guidetti et al., FOTON-M3 / <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5705745/">tardigrades in space research</a>.</li>
          <li>Anhydrobiosis and extremes: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-animal-021419-083711">Arakawa 2020</a> (tun, −272.8 °C, 7.5 GPa, radiation).</li>
          <li>Vent film: NOAA Ocean Exploration, 2016 Deepwater Exploration of the Marianas, public domain.</li>
          <li>Jelly film: NOAA Okeanos Explorer, <i>Crossota millsae</i>, public domain.</li>
          <li>Essay: Elliott Staffer, <a href={ESSAY}>MÎŘRŒRÄ (mirrora)</a>, 18–19 Oct 2026, Venice CA. Also the <a href={DROPBOX}>Dropbox file</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  import { startMirrora } from '../scripts/mirrora';
  const root = document.getElementById('mr-root');
  if (root) startMirrora(root);
</script>

<style>
  .mr-hero {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    align-items: center;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #000;
  }
  .mr-hero-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2.75rem);
    align-items: center;
    padding-top: clamp(6.5rem, 14vw, 9rem);
    padding-bottom: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }
  .mr-hero-stage {
    margin: 0;
    border-radius: 28px;
  }
  .mr-hero-stage canvas {
    height: min(46vh, 420px);
    touch-action: pan-y;
    cursor: default;
  }
  .mr-hero h1 em {
    background: var(--aura);
    background-size: 300% 100%;
    animation: aura-flow var(--aura-flow-dur, 8s) linear infinite;
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    background-clip: text;
    color: transparent;
  }
  .mr-cards {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
  }
  .mr-cards.two {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
  .mr-cards article {
    padding: 1.25rem 1.2rem;
    border-radius: 1.15rem;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  }
  .mr-cards h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 1.08rem;
  }
  .mr-cards p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-size: 0.94rem;
  }
  .mr-vid {
    margin: 2.2rem 0 0;
  }
  .mr-vid video {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: 22px;
    background: #000;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  }
  .mr-vid figcaption,
  .mr-stage figcaption,
  .mr-cap,
  .mr-src {
    color: var(--text-faint);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .mr-vid figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
  }
  .mr-stage {
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0;
    border-radius: 22px;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    background: #03040a;
  }
  .mr-stage canvas {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: min(52vh, 420px);
    touch-action: none;
    cursor: grab;
  }
  .mr-stage.tall canvas {
    height: min(56vh, 460px);
  }
  .mr-hero .mr-hero-stage {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .mr-hero .mr-hero-stage canvas {
    height: min(46vh, 420px);
    touch-action: pan-y;
    cursor: default;
  }
  .mr-stage figcaption,
  .mr-cap {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0.7rem 1rem 0.9rem;
  }
  .mr-pair {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .mr-code {
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.3rem;
    border-radius: 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    background: #0b0d12;
    overflow: auto;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: #cfe9ff;
  }
  .mr-src {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  .mr-src li {
    margin: 0.45rem 0;
  }
  .mr-page i {
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .mr-life {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.75rem);
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  }
  .mr-life > h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 2.4vw, 1.7rem);
  }
  .mr-page .band {
    background: #0b0b0d;
  }
  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .mr-hero-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1.05fr 0.95fr;
    }
    .mr-hero-stage {
      order: -1;
    }
    .mr-hero .mr-hero-stage canvas {
      height: min(68vh, 620px);
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 880px) {
    .mr-pair {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
    .mr-split {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 0.9fr;
      gap: 2.5rem;
      align-items: start;
    }
    .mr-cards.two {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
